A||||13||FIEROCIOUS||Best alighment specs?||||5||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||07-23-2000||02:53 PM||||I recently had my Fiero alighned.I used specs from an internet sight for SCCA Fiero's. The car was WAY twitchy at speeds above 80 MPH (I did this speed legaly) and I think the specs I used are to blame.<P>What specs should I use as a good compromise from low speed manuverability to high speed stability? What could benifit by changing caster if anything? Is there a way to lessen bump steer (other than bump steer kit?)<P>Current specs:<BR>Front: <BR>Camber 0 degrees<BR>Caster 7 degrees I think? Stock.<BR>0 toe in total<P>Rear:<BR>Camber 1.5 degrees<BR>1/8 toe in total ||||reg||5
Z||000001||FIEROCIOUS||07-23-2000||02:54 PM||||Um... there's an N in alighnment dugh!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||07-23-2000||04:07 PM||||Theres no H in Alignment either.<P>I think you need more negative camber<P>rear toe should be 0 to 1/16 in||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||07-23-2000||09:44 PM||||You might want to double check everything,those numbers are what I came up with also,and work great.<BR>Do you have a rear sway bar?||||reg||1
Z||000004||gixxer||07-24-2000||01:05 AM||||Surely the rear camber is -1.5 degrees?...<BR>(stock is -1) If it's +1.5, its bound to get a bit lively! ||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||07-24-2000||03:45 AM||||sorry -1.5 degrees camber and yes I do have a rear sway bar. also lowered 2"||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||07-24-2000||10:13 AM||||Having the car get twitchy at higher speeds is the side effect of the tight rear suspension,which gives the low speed agility.<BR>Either you're running out of travel in the back,or more likely,the swaybar is just too much with the springs you have,the cars from the factory that have a swaybar,have much softer springs.I just put a swaybar on the rear of my car that's only a third as stiff as the one on '88 year cars,yet it may come off soon if the rear breakaway is too rapid,I haven't found any good places to try it out yet.  <BR>One thing you might try is to get a pair of very sticky,soft rear tires,Bridgestone RE71s or equivelent.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||07-24-2000||01:08 PM||||i had mine aligned to OEM specs, front and rear swaybars, Bilstien rear struts and front shocks, Its very stable at very high (illegal) speed. Only gets a little twitchy in tight turns at 2 or 3 times speed warning signs, use throttle to steer. I love it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||86_Fastback||07-24-2000||04:37 PM||||rogergarrison,<P>I just rebuilt my suspension a few months ago, but the KYB shocks and struts are do not damp enough.  Could you give me your impressions on the Bilstiens and the part numbers.  I was going to go with them to try to solve by problem.<P>Gregg<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||FIEROCIOUS||07-24-2000||06:15 PM||||I would also like more info on the bilsteins.<P>LowCG, you got me thinking, When I lowered the car I didn't adjust the swaybar end links to make the bar horizontal. I did this while I rebuilt the engine so maybe I need to see what happens? <P>KYB are great on the front but the rears are a bit soft. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||FIEROCIOUS||07-24-2000||06:22 PM||||Any thoughts on caster?<BR>I plan on useing BFG's race tire next year. How do they compair to Bridgestones?<P>P.S. rear springs 300lb front 275lb<BR>I'm getting close to perfect balance and neutral steer. Thanks for all your help.||||reg||1
Z||000011||PJ||07-24-2000||10:42 PM||||The more front caster, the more the car will want to track straight. It will also make turning the steering wheel more of an effort. You want to balance the straight ahead stability while not having to work too hard in the turns. Seven degrees pos. caster seems excessive to me. After 12 years of playing around with alignment #'s this is what I currently use:<P>Front: 4.5 to 5 deg. pos. caster, .5 to 1 deg. neg. camber, usually a little bit of toe-in (1/16").<P>Rear: 1.5 to 2 deg. neg. camber, ALWAYS a little bit of toe-in.<P>Good alignment work is not easy and is time consuming. All too often the customer is sent away with an alignment that is "close enough." Unless you have some confirmation of what the final #'s are you can't be sure of getting what you paid for. The latest computer controlled alignment rack is only as good as the guy working it and the last time is was calibrated. A friend of mine gets excellent repeatable results with turnplates, analog gauges and a toe-stick.||||reg||1
Z||000012||FIEROCIOUS||07-25-2000||11:32 AM||||I have a good buddy who will do it for me!<BR>He owns the Cougar in Dusk 'til Dawn that george cloney drove.<BR>Does 1/16 mean total toe in or each side?<BR>What is the stock caster on the Fiero?<BR>PJ thanks for the info!||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||07-26-2000||01:10 AM||||I found that the car actually stuck better with less rear toe(1/16" totall),yet was easier to throw around,more predictable,with 1/8" rear toe,totall.<BR>The swaybar will be better if level,but unless it's exteme,or binding,it shouldn't make a whole lot of difference that way.<BR>I just put my new swaybar on the back that I bought from Saner Performance,and it works pretty good,still will understeer slightly in a nuetral throttle,steady corner.I'll get some pics here next time I'm underneath,it's mounted with heim joints on the ends,is 3/4" diameter chromoly steel,with a short(8") arm,and mounts to the control arms as far outboard as possible.||||reg||1
